\title[Hyperbolic geometry of the Pythagorean tree]{Hyperbolic Geometry of the Pythagorean Tree:\\ A Complete Census of the Straight Lines}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{7 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Every primitive Pythagorean triple arises from a unique \emph{Euclid seed} $(m,n)$ with $0<n<m$, $\gcd(m,n)=1$ and $m+n$ odd, and the seeds are organised by three unimodular moves into an infinite ternary tree. Embedding a seed as the point $z(m,n)=(n+i)/m$ of the hyperbolic upper half-plane and transporting the picture to the Poincar\'e disk with $i$ at the centre produces a plot visibly filled with straight rays. We explain that phenomenon completely.

We prove a master metric identity, $\cosh d(z(m,n),z(m',n'))=1+\big((nm'-n'm)^2+(m-m')^2\big)/(2mm')$, and deduce a \emph{ring theorem}: a seed of hypotenuse $c$ lies at distance strictly between $\frac12\log c$ and $\frac12\log 2c$ from the centre. A collinearity calculus based on the hyperbolic Cayley--Menger invariant $\Phi$ is developed, together with an \emph{arithmetic bridge} expressing $\Phi$ for seed nodes as the square of an integer $3\times3$ determinant divided by $(2m_1m_2m_3)^2$; consequently straightness is quantised. Collinearity with the centre is shown to be equality of the \emph{radial invariant} $\rad(m,n)=(m^2-n^2-1)/(mn)$, whose level sets are the conics $bm^2-amn-bn^2=b$. Each such line carries an explicit automorphism driven by a solution of $s^2-asu-b^2u^2=1$, acting as a hyperbolic translation of length $\arcosh(s-au/2)$. For integral $\rad=k$ we classify all positive integral points, obtain distance quantisation in multiples of $2\log\lambda_k$ with $\lambda_k$ the $k$-th metallic ratio, and prove a parity census, pencil separation, quadratic-irrational ideal endpoints, exact ball counts, hypotenuse growth $\lambda_k^{4j}/2<c_j<\lambda_k^{4j}$ and a metallic gap. Finally, a rational line is nonempty iff it is infinite iff its discriminant $a^2+4b^2$ is not a perfect square; as a corollary $(m^2-n^2-1)^2+(2mn)^2$ is never a perfect square, whence every node lies on exactly one infinite line through the centre. Two negative results complete the picture: horocycles carry only finitely many seeds, and the tree's middle spine, though visually straight, has Gram defect exactly $1$.
\end{abstract}

